Flexbox child properties

LIST OF FLEXBOX CHILD PROPERTIES
  1. order
  2. flex-grow
  3. flex-shrink
  4. flex-basis
  5. flex  (shorthand of grow + shrink + basis)
  6. align-self
  7. margin: auto  (bonus trick for flex items)
  8. min-width / max-width (bonus - limits for flex items)

1. order: Changes the VISUAL order of an item without changing the HTML order.

Default value: 0
Accepted values: any integer (positive or negative)

How it works:
  Items are displayed from the lowest order value to the highest.
  Items with the same value follow the HTML source order.

Example:
  .item1 { order: 3; }
  .item2 { order: 1; }
  .item3 { order: 2; }
  Visual result: item2, item3, item1

2. flex-grow: Decides how much an item should GROW to fill extra empty space in the container.

Default value: 0  (do not grow)
Accepted values: any number >= 0 (no units)

How it works:
  Extra free space is shared among items in proportion to their flex-grow values.

Example:
  .item1 { flex-grow: 1; }
  .item2 { flex-grow: 2; }
  .item3 { flex-grow: 1; }
  Free space is divided 1 : 2 : 1.
  item2 gets twice as much of the extra space as item1 or item3.

3. flex-shrink: Decides how much an item should SHRINK when there is not enough space in the container.

Default value: 1  (items can shrink)
Accepted values: any number >= 0

How it works:
  When the total item size is bigger than the container, items shrink
  in proportion to their flex-shrink values.
  A higher value shrinks faster. 0 means it will never shrink.

Example:
  .item1 { flex-shrink: 1; }
  .item2 { flex-shrink: 0; }   /* keeps its size, never shrinks */
  .item3 { flex-shrink: 3; }   /* shrinks 3x faster */

4. flex-basis: ets the STARTING (initial) size of an item along the main axis,
  before growing or shrinking happens.

Default value: auto  (uses the item's width/height or its content size)
Accepted values: length (px, %, rem, em ...), auto, content, 0

How it works:
  - If flex-direction is row     -> flex-basis acts like width
  - If flex-direction is column  -> flex-basis acts like height
  - flex-basis overrides width/height on the main axis (when it is not auto)

Example:
  .item1 { flex-basis: 200px; }
  .item2 { flex-basis: 30%; }
  .item3 { flex-basis: auto; }

5. flex  (shorthand):A shorthand that sets flex-grow, flex-shrink and flex-basis together.

Syntax:
  flex: <flex-grow> <flex-shrink> <flex-basis>;

Default value: 0 1 auto

Common shorthand values:
  flex: 1;           ->  1 1 0%      (grow equally, shrink, start from 0)
  flex: auto;        ->  1 1 auto    (grow and shrink from content size)
  flex: none;        ->  0 0 auto    (fixed, no grow, no shrink)
  flex: 0 0 200px;   ->  fixed 200px item
  flex: 2;           ->  2 1 0%      (takes twice the share of flex: 1)

Example:
  .sidebar { flex: 0 0 250px; }   /* fixed 250px */
  .content { flex: 1; }           /* takes all remaining space */

6. align-self: Overrides the parent's align-items for ONE individual item,
  controlling its alignment on the CROSS axis.

Default value: auto  (inherits the parent's align-items)
Accepted values:
  auto | flex-start | flex-end | center | baseline | stretch

Meaning of each value (for flex-direction: row, cross axis = vertical):
  auto        -> follows the parent's align-items
  flex-start  -> item sits at the top
  flex-end    -> item sits at the bottom
  center      -> item is vertically centered
  baseline    -> item aligns to the text baseline of other items
  stretch     -> item stretches to fill the container height
                 (only if the item has no fixed height)

Example:
  .container { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; height: 300px; }
  .item2     { align-self: center; }
  .item3     { align-self: flex-end; }

  Result: item1 at top, item2 in the middle, item3 at the bottom.

7. BONUS: margin: auto on flex items
Auto margins absorb all free space in that direction, pushing items apart. This is the way to align a single item on the main axis.

Example:
  .nav { display: flex; }
  .nav .logo   { }
  .nav .login  { margin-left: auto; }

  Result: logo on the left, login button pushed to the far right.

Other forms:
  margin: auto;        -> centers the item both horizontally and vertically
  margin-top: auto;    -> pushes item to the bottom (in a column)
  margin-right: auto;  -> pushes following items to the right

8. BONUS: min-width, max-width (and min-height, max-height)
Sets limits that flex-grow and flex-shrink can't cross.

Important concept:
  By default, a flex item has min-width: auto, which means it will not shrink smaller than its content. Long text or images can cause overflow.

Fix:
  .item { min-width: 0; }    /* allows the item to shrink below content size */

Example:
  .card { flex: 1; min-width: 200px; max-width: 400px; }

PARENT vs CHILD PROPERTIES - SUMMARY TABLE
==========================================================

  Property        Applied on   Axis         Purpose
  --------------  -----------  -----------  ---------------------------------
  order           child        -            Change visual order
  flex-grow       child        main         Share extra space
  flex-shrink     child        main         Shrink when space is short
  flex-basis      child        main         Starting size
  flex            child        main         Shorthand for grow/shrink/basis
  align-self      child        cross        Align one item individually
  margin: auto    child        both         Push / center a single item
  min/max-width   child        main         Size limits

  justify-content parent       main         Align all items on main axis
  align-items     parent       cross        Align all items on cross axis
  align-content   parent       cross        Align wrapped lines
  flex-wrap       parent       -            Allow items to wrap
  gap             parent       -            Space between items
